  "fashion": {
    "Overview": "The costume styles can be roughly divided into three categories according to the story background: China before Qing, Qing Dynasty, and Republican China. The latter two has generally historically accurate costumes, while stories set in ancient China have more imaginative costumes. The following paragraphs describes Wuxia costumes in settings before Qing, which is symbolised by jiaoling youren. Costume and makeup in Wuxia productions blend traditional Chinese clothing with stylized adaptations for action and character expression. Common traits include layered silhouettes and functional elements that allow free movement for fight scenes. Colors, textures, and accessories sometimes symbolize…",
    "Weapons": "Chinese medieval weapons are used. The hero usually uses a jian sword, their companions might use broadswords, spears, or bows. Qi-gong requires fighting with palms open instead of holding fists. The villain probably also uses a sword, but his minions might use battle axes, maces, poisoned darts and meteor hammers (流星锤). Weapons are decorated with red tassel. Mythical creatures probably won't show up, but they appear on weapons and scabbards, along with abstract cloud patterns and inscriptions…",
    "Tops": "Layered Hanfu jiaoling (交领, lit. 'cross-collar') collars; Rolling up narrow sleeves allow combat mobility; Wide sleeves and long clothes hints a character skilled in Qing-gong; Frog buttons appear in Qing or Republican settings.",
    "Bottoms": "Loose trousers, usually tapered leg; Trouser legs are fastend with puttee wraps or tucked into boots,",
    "Outerwear": "Brotherhood of Blades (2014), where the protagonists are Jinyiwei",
    "Shoes": "Boots, sometimes iron-tipped; the zao-xue (皂靴) are black boots with white sole worn by government officals; cloth shoes, sometimes with cross-bound straps; straw sandals and wooden clogs",
    "Accessories": "Belts: cloth sashes, leather belts with metal hook, silk cords; Wrist guards (leather/metal), bangles, finger sheaths; Jade pendants, pouch, gourd (containing liquds or pills), knife sheath; all hung on the belt; face veils, eye patches, masks; Li (笠) hats; the most symbolic hat of Wuxia; Wei-mao (帷帽, lit. 'veiled hat'), worn by mysterious women; Guan (冠) hats; has various styles and identifies…",
    "Hair and Makeup": "The Shaolin Temple (1982), where the protagonist is a Buddhist monk Both men and women have long hair, often tied into a bun or a ponytail; (not cutting one's hair is considered \" filial piety \" in the Confucian ideology)"
  },
